            <ArticleTitle>Potential of Baliospermum montanum against compound 48/80-induced systemic anaphylaxis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1213-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/13880201003677827</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Decoctions of Baliospermum montanum Müll. Arg. (Euphorbiaceae) leaves are reported to be useful in the treatment of asthma and other respiratory complications in the Ayurvedic system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the mast cell stabilization and antihistaminic activities of the chloroform (BMLC) and ethanol (BMLE) extracts of the leaves of Baliospermum montanum.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The stabilization potential was studied on mouse peritoneal mast cells and the antihistaminic activity was carried out by determining the mortality rate of mice treated with toxicant (compound 48/80) and the effect on elevation of histamine release upon degranulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The increased number of intact mast cells (43.640 ± 1.7% and 61.57 ± 1.79% at 200 and 400 mg/ kg, respectively) suggested that the BMLC stabilized the mast cell degranulation and showed decreased elevation of histamine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BMLC extract was found to be most effective against degranulation and release of histamine from mast cells. Identifying the lead from this plant will be a definite target for treating allergic diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
